            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Adam Smith's Early Life</strong><br>\nAdam Smith was born in 1723 in Kirkcaldy, Scotland. His father died before he was born, and his mother raised him. He attended the University of Glasgow at age 14, studying moral philosophy. After Glasgow, he won a scholarship to Oxford University, but he found the curriculum unstimulating.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Introduction to Economics</strong><br>\nSmith's interest in economics emerged while at Glasgow, under the mentorship of Francis Hutcheson. He was influenced by the ideas of the Enlightenment, which emphasized reason and individualism over tradition. Smith's lectures covered a range of topics, including rhetoric, jurisprudence, and the economy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The Theory of Moral Sentiments\"</strong><br>\nIn 1759, Smith published \"The Theory of Moral Sentiments.\" This work delved into how human morality depends on sympathy between agent and observer. This book laid the groundwork for his later economic theories by examining the ways moral considerations affect market behavior.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Professor at Glasgow</strong><br>\nReturning to Glasgow in 1751, Smith took the Chair of Logic and later moved to the Chair of Moral Philosophy. His lectures were popular, and he integrated his economic ideas with moral philosophy, discussing how economic freedom could lead to moral progress.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The Wealth of Nations\"</strong><br>\nThe Wealth of Nations, published in 1776, is Smith's most famous work. It argued that free-market economies are more productive and beneficial to their societies. The book described his theory of the \"invisible hand,\" which helps supply and demand reach equilibrium.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Laissez-Faire Economics</strong><br>\nSmith advocated for laissez-faire economic policy, which suggests that government should interfere as little as possible in the direction of economic affairs. He believed that when individuals pursue their own interest, they inadvertently contribute to the economic well-being of society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
