          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Division of Labor</strong><br>\nOne of Smith's key contributions to economics was his concept of the division of labor. He explained that dividing production processes into specialized tasks increases efficiency and prosperity. This idea remains a fundamental principle of modern industry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nSmith's ideas have had a profound influence on modern economics and social theory. His work paved the way for future economists and was a cornerstone of classical liberalism. \"The Wealth of Nations\" remains a key text in the study of economics and capitalism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Death</strong><br>\nSmith spent the later part of his life revising his earlier works and contributing to the literature on law and economics. He died in 1790 in Edinburgh, leaving behind a legacy as the father of modern economics. His ideas continue to be taught and valued for their historical importance and relevance.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
